cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Code for "Deciding when to decide: how recency, urgency, risk, and bias shape
  human sequential decision-making, a case study across the obsessive-compulsive
  spectrum"
message: If you use this software, please cite the paper below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Ahmed H.
    family-names: Abdelrazik
    affiliation: >-
      Max Planck Institute for Biological Cybernetics, Tübingen, Germany; and
      University of Tübingen, Tübingen, Germany
  - given-names: Peter
    family-names: Dayan
    affiliation: >-
      Max Planck Institute for Biological Cybernetics, Tübingen, Germany; and
      University of Tübingen, Tübingen, Germany
license: MIT
repository-code: https://github.com/aabdelrazik/deciding-when-to-decide
abstract: >-
  Models of when people stop gathering evidence and commit, built as a partially
  observable Markov decision process with interpretable departures from
  optimality: a biased prior, transient exaggeration of the newest evidence,
  progressive forgetting of older evidence, an inflated cost of being wrong,
  patience and urgency, and misperception of the deadline. Includes the fitting
  pipeline, the model and parameter recovery analyses, the regression
  comparisons, and the scripts that produce every figure and table in the paper.
keywords:
  - sequential decision-making
  - evidence accumulation
  - POMDP
  - computational psychiatry
  - obsessive-compulsive disorder
  - model comparison
